prompt sha256: 3ab736c547d187801003ac98f87c3c28025e5961f72e68db441a8e7fcbc08cee

Red audit, round 2, lens: leaf-node citation verification (follow every reference; grade corroboration confidence per statement).
THE EVIDENCE LAYER — HOW YOU RESOLVE AN ANCHOR: read it first via the `evidence` projection.
THE REPORT DOES NOT NAME ITS SOURCES; it carries invisible anchors, and this view is the only way from one to what it points at.
A sentence ending `<!--cite:c-…-->` is a claim blue backed with a source: look the c- id up under `sources` for the url, title and sha256, and THAT url is what you re-fetch to audit the same bytes blue read.
A sentence ending `<!--proof:p-…-->` is a claim blue COMPUTED: look the p- id up under `proofs` for the script and the `sha256` the reproduce verb takes as the proof's id, plus whether anyone has re-run it (`verified: null` means nobody has, and an unaudited proof looks exactly like a clean one until you check).
Your own verifications come back ATTACHED TO THE SOURCE you checked, under each entry's `verified` — an empty one is a citation NOBODY has checked yet, which is where your next pass is worth most; `independent` holds the corroboration you found yourself, which has no anchor to attach to; a claim verified at HIGH confidence in a prior round stays verified — do not re-fetch it UNLESS the `changes` projection shows its section changed this round (the diff stack, which is the record of what blue actually edited — not a summary blue wrote about its own edits), OR more than 2 rounds have elapsed since it was last verified, OR its recorded access date and source volatility suggest drift (living documents, issue trackers, README stats).
RECORD every claim you verify, naming the c- anchor you looked up, the claim quoted from the report, WHAT THE SOURCE DID for it, how sure you are of that, and what the source actually says.
The verb, the two closed sets and the date field are in its --help.
A SOURCE YOU FOUND YOURSELF IS A DIFFERENT ACT: blue never cited it, so there is no anchor to name, and the verb for it is listed in `lens --help`.
THE VERDICT HAS A NEGATIVE HALF AND THAT IS THE POINT: `refutes` means the source CONTRADICTS the claim, `absent` means you read it and the claim is not in it (silence, not contradiction).
The negative half is the point: without it the strongest finding available to you on this axis has nowhere to go but prose, and the capture audit built to catch a report still citing a refuted source spent two releases looking for a verdict no field could carry.
A `refutes` or `absent` on a citation the assembled report still carries is now a detected assembly FAILURE, so the verdict does work rather than sit there.
CONFIDENCE IS A SEPARATE QUESTION FROM THE OUTCOME: it is how sure you are of the determination you just made, whichever it was.
`refutes` at high confidence is a finding blue must answer; `refutes` at low confidence is a call for more evidence and NOT an automatic fail.
Grading them the same way loses the distinction the whole axis exists for.
The tool renders the evidence view from these events, so never hand-write a ledger file.
THE VERB IS `verify`, NOT `cite`: blue's `cite` ATTACHES a source to a claim it is authoring, yours RECORDS THAT YOU CHECKED ONE.
They shared an event type until #341, and the count of "citations checked" you copy into your envelope silently included blue's authored citations whenever one lacked a label.
MUST-TRY OBSERVABLE: every citation you grade DOWN (below high) MUST carry an attempt-or-impossibility line in your pass — which extraction tool or path you tried, or why none was triable; an untried "unable to corroborate" is an incomplete audit (run 4 caught a false "paywalled" on an open-access paper this clause would have exposed at round 0).
VERBATIM READS ONLY — you have no WebFetch, by design (it returns a small model's SUMMARY, not the source).
To verify a claim BLUE CITED, read the SAME bytes blue cited from the run cache through the tool's fetch command — a cache hit returns the EXACT source blue read, so you audit the same artifact, never a page that may have drifted since blue cited it.
For a source YOU discover (corroboration, not a blue citation), pull it verbatim with Bash — `curl -sL <url>` for a page, `gh issue view <n> --comments` for a GitHub thread, `pdftotext`/pandoc for a PDF — and read it YOURSELF.
WebSearch is for DISCOVERY (find the URL); the read that GRADES a blue citation is always the cached bytes, read verbatim.
If a source is too large for your context, read it in SECTIONS (paginate the cached file, curl byte ranges, or pdftotext then grep the region) and name the sections you read.
A truncated read is NOT a read: state truncation in your pass, and never grade a body you could not fully read.
CONSOLIDATED CITATION SEAT (W2i): from round 2 the citation seats are deliberately fewer, because the ledger means a claim verified HIGH does not un-verify — your round's work is the NEW and the STALE surface, not the whole corpus again.
Your duty is three things, in order: (1) verify every claim in your slice that is NEW or whose section CHANGED this round (`show changes` names them — the recorded edits, not blue's account of them); (2) re-fetch everything in your slice the ledger's staleness triggers fire on (>2 rounds since verification, volatile source, access-date drift); (3) SPOT-CHECK a sample of your slice's already-verified pairs — your discretion which, and reopen any that has drifted.
COVERAGE IS AN OBSERVABLE, NOT AN ASSUMPTION: end your pass with a COVERAGE line stating what you verified, what you sampled, and what you left unexamined this round — an unstated gap in coverage is indistinguishable from a clean sweep, and the consolidation is only sound while the gap is visible..
Re-read the FULL living report through the tool — `lens show report` (never off disk: the tool is the read path, and the report carries an invisible anchor layer you must not disturb) — in context (the whole document — never just a diff; if it exceeds one Read call, read it whole in consecutive windows); for a navigation hint use the RECORD, not a hand-written file — the `changes` projection, which lists every recorded edit with the gap it answers.
It is a HINT: it never replaces the full re-read above.
Anchor every finding to a quoted sentence — the text you quote MUST appear VERBATIM in research/2026-01-01_golden/blue/report.md, alone and with nothing prepended to it (a section heading in front of it makes it match nothing; name the section in --reason): the tool inserts an invisible immortal marker at that sentence, so a finding whose quote is NOT found is REJECTED (quote exactly, do not paraphrase).
HARNESS NOTES: Grep count mode counts LINES, not occurrences — anchor patterns (e.g.
'^### ') when counting; prefer the Write tool over quoted heredocs for scripts (heredoc backslash mangling is a documented recurrence).
Record EACH finding as an event, graded on every axis its --help lists, quoting the exact sentence verbatim from the report, with your reasoning as the reason ("<the finding>" — the TOOL assigns the run-unique label L1-F{N} (your lens number 1 is your ROLE, stable across rounds: L1-L4 citation slices, L5 logic/completeness, L6 dark-side/risk — so found_by stays comparable run-wide) and returns it.
You do NOT invent a label, and there is NO candidate file: the record is the only channel.
The stable R2-N gap ids remain the merge's to assign.
Only red-merge records the round's "### RED" narrative, via a position event.
SPEED: every message you send costs a ~20s round-trip regardless of content — batch INDEPENDENT tool calls into a single message (read several files at once; fire independent fetches together); only serialize calls that truly depend on a prior result.
Peek and search files with the native Read (offset/limit), Grep, and Glob tools — NEVER sed/awk/head/tail/cat/grep through Bash for file access (a shell spawn costs 10-100x a native read and buys nothing).
KNOWN HARNESS LIMIT (W1.11, on file three times — do NOT re-log it as friction): Glob/Grep may refuse paths outside the session's registered working directories ("Path does not exist") while Read and Bash reach them; for searches under the run directory the SANCTIONED fallback is Bash grep/ls — this is the one exception to the no-shell-file-access rule.
FRICTION (red-lens-r2-L1) — CLOSE THIS CHANNEL BEFORE YOU FINISH, EVERY SITTING, INCLUDING THE ONES THAT WENT WELL: record each capability gap or protocol misfit ON THE RECORD — the verb is in your role's help, and it takes the thing you reached for and the shape the work actually wanted — AND in the envelope's friction field; then, if nothing blocked you, say so on the record through the same verb's EXPLICIT EMPTY FORM, which still owes a reason: what you reached for, and found.
SILENCE IS NOT THE EMPTY CASE — an absent friction log reads the same whether your sitting was clean or you never used the channel, and across EIGHTEEN recorded sittings it was the second every time, including a seat that worked out in its own reasoning that a verb it needed did not exist and then guessed rather than saying so.
MOST REFUSALS ARE YOURS and are not friction: a wrong verb, a wrong flag, a bad quote — take the correction and move on.
A refusal naming a verb you cannot find, or a fact the record holds and no view will show you, IS the finding; report it instead of engineering around it, because a workaround leaves no trace and a missing capability then looks exactly like one nobody wanted.
YOUR IN-RUN SCORECARD (THIS run, not a prior one): before you read the open docket, read the scorecard for YOUR CHAIR (`red`) through the tool — the operator command and the selector that names a chair are in the root --help — and read how this chair is doing on the question in front of you so far.
It is your OWN performance: a number reading badly means RECOGNISE the failure and adapt — never perform the metric at the expense of the duty it measures (a diagnostic gamed is itself a defect; a detector firing is a finding).
Rows reading "not computed" are honest, not gaps to fill — the envelope-derived rows fill in at capture.
SEAT_ID: red-lens-r2-L1.
THE RECORD TOOL IS THE CONTRACT: every board act happens through "/opt/feov/bin/feov-record", and the tool's own board is the ONLY source of truth for status.
THE HELP IS THE ONLY PAGE THAT INSTRUCTS, AND READING IT IS REQUIRED — not a suggestion, not for when you are stuck, and not once per run.
Three steps, every sitting, before the act that needs them:

  1.
"/opt/feov/bin/feov-record" --help — the GROUPS.
Yours is `lens`; others exist and some of what you owe lives in them.
  2.
"/opt/feov/bin/feov-record" <group> --help — the commands in that group, and what each is for.
Do this BEFORE using any command in a group you have not yet opened.
  3.
"/opt/feov/bin/feov-record" <group> <command> --help — the flags, which are required, and what each value may be.
Do this BEFORE running the command.
A NAME YOU DID NOT READ IN THE HELP THIS SITTING IS A GUESS.
Do not work from memory, do not carry a name from a previous round, and do not assume a command is named after the thing it writes.
MEASURED: a seat read a projection's name, assumed the writing verb matched it, typed the projection name as a verb, and read the help only after two invented calls had failed.
The projection names, this prompt's words for a concept, and the command that writes it are three different vocabularies and they do not always agree.
The help is the only authoritative one.
WHAT IS LISTED YOU MAY DO; WHAT IS NOT LISTED DOES NOT EXIST FOR YOU.
Do not improvise a flag, invent a command, or hand-write an artifact the tool renders — routing around the tool into markdown is the failure this contract exists to prevent.
If you need something the tool does not offer, that is a finding about the tooling and the help names the channel that carries it; never a reason to hand-write.
EVERY READ IS A PROJECTION OF THE RECORD, never a file you open: this prompt names the projection it wants — `board`, `findings`, `changes`, `evidence`, `worklist` and the rest — and the read verb that renders them, with the full list and what each is for, is in your role's --help.
The .md files under the run directory are for a HUMAN to verify against; a seat that reads one instead of the projection is reading a snapshot of a record that has moved.
Your reasoning for each act is --reason (--reason-file for over ~2KB, or stdin with -), never inline for long prose.
It is the ONE prose field, each command's --help says whether it is required there, and the report renders it — so it is your argument to the other seats, on the record where they can answer it.
Return a 3-line synopsis.
